13:09:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That documentary detail is actually a good catch, and you're right that it counts for something—orbital distance genuinely *does* affect Earth's temperature. The key is *what timescale* it operates on. **What the documentary was likely describing** This is probably related to **Milankovitch cycles**—real, well-established astronomical patterns that include: - **Eccentricity**: how circular vs. elliptical Earth's orbit is, changing over a ~100,000-year cycle - **Axial tilt**: the tilt angle itself varies between about 22.1° and 24.5° over a ~41,000-year cycle - **Precession**: the direction Earth's axis points slowly rotates over about 26,000 years, which changes *when* in the orbit perihelion occurs These cycles are a big deal in climate science—they're linked to the timing of ice ages and warm periods over hundreds of thousands of years. So distance-related orbital effects are absolutely real science, not a myth at all. **The critical distinction: millennia vs. months** Where it gets confused is the *timescale*: - Milankovitch cycles cause temperature shifts over **thousands to hundreds of thousands of years** — they're why we have ice ages - Summer vs. winter happens over **one year**, driven overwhelmingly by axial tilt (the angle effect), not by Earth's position in its orbit So the documentary wasn't wrong—orbital mechanics really do affect climate over deep time. It's just a completely different phenomenon from why it's hot in July versus January. Documentaries covering both topics in the same episode (which is common, since they're both "orbit-related") can easily blur together in memory into one big "orbit/distance = temperature" idea, even though they're operating on wildly different timescales for different reasons. So you weren't picking up nothing—you were picking up something real, just from a different part of the puzzle than the one that explains seasons.
